    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Two months of tiredness, easy bruising and bleeding gums, with pancytopenia and 12 percent blasts on the blood smear; acute leukemia suspected and urgent hematology review booked</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>D61.818</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting concern at the index visit, superseded by the bone marrow diagnosis two days later; its timing echoes the documented decline in her health (D).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Acute myeloid leukemia diagnosed on bone marrow biopsy (34 percent myeloblasts), in a physicist with decades of occupational exposure to ionizing radiation; synthetic modern diagnosis linked to her documented leukemia</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C92.00</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis and subtype; the type of her real leukemia is not documented.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Occupational exposure to ionizing radiation over about four decades: X-ray work early in her career, laboratory work with polonium and other radioactive sources, and a laboratory accident in which a sealed polonium source ruptured on her bench about ten years ago</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z57.1</td><td>S — Synthetic modern coding of her documented occupational exposure.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Pulmonary tuberculosis in her forties, treated to completion; no symptoms of active disease</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z86.11</td><td>S — Synthetic modern coding of her documented tuberculosis; the treatment details are synthetic.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

