<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute myeloid leukemia (C92.00)</content>. The marrow aspirate shows 34 percent myeloblasts with dysplasia in the red cell and platelet lines, and flow cytometry shows myeloid blasts (CD34, CD117, CD13, CD33, HLA-DR and MPO positive): acute myeloid leukemia. Normal INR and fibrinogen, the blast phenotype, and a negative rapid PML::RARA test exclude acute promyelocytic leukemia. Cytogenetics and the molecular panel will take about ten days; classification, risk and the treatment choice depend on them. Dr. Engquist-Mensah and I agree. [S]<list><item>Cytogenetics and molecular panel from the marrow; review when back.</item><item>Echocardiogram, ECG, and lung function tests with diffusing capacity on the day of the results review, given her past tuberculosis.</item><item>HLA typing sent.</item><item>Hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV tests sent before therapy.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Past pulmonary tuberculosis (Z86.11)</content>. Chest X-ray today shows old fibrotic scarring at the right apex and no sign of active disease; she completed treatment. Treated tuberculosis does not need preventive therapy, but reactivation is possible during neutropenia, so a new cough, fever or night sweats will be investigated promptly. [S]<list><item>Lung function tests with diffusing capacity before treatment.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Occupational exposure to ionizing radiation (Z57.1)</content>. Her account is recorded in full; leukemia after long exposure to ionizing radiation is well recognized. The dose records will show the extent of her monitored exposure. [S]<list><item>Written request for dosimetry and bioassay records to the employer&apos;s radiation safety office, as the regulations entitle her.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count with differential and smear</td><td>At diagnosis; two to three times a week during induction; before each cycle</td><td>Completed today</td><td>White cells 1.9, neutrophils 0.5, hemoglobin 7.1 g/dL, platelets 22 x10^3/uL, blasts 14% on the smear</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Bone marrow: morphology, flow cytometry, cytogenetics, molecular panel</td><td>At diagnosis; day 21 to 28 of cycle 1; then as response and transplant planning require</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Aspirate 34% myeloblasts with multilineage dysplasia; flow cytometry myeloid; cytogenetics and molecular panel sent</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Coagulation tests (INR, fibrinogen) to exclude the coagulopathy of acute promyelocytic leukemia</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>INR 1.1, fibrinogen 310 mg/dL: no coagulopathy; rapid PML::RARA negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Chemistry: electrolytes, creatinine, calcium, phosphate, uric acid, LDH, liver tests</td><td>At diagnosis; during venetoclax ramp-up for tumor lysis; then weekly in cycle 1 and before each cycle</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal kidney and liver tests two days ago; LDH 412 U/L and uric acid 6.4 mg/dL; repeat on the first treatment day</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fitness: performance status, comorbidity (HCT-CI), echocardiogram, lung function</td><td>Before choosing induction</td><td>Ordered</td><td>ECOG 1 two days ago; echocardiogram, ECG and lung function tests booked for the results review</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Transfusion support: thresholds, irradiated and leukoreduced products</td><td>At each blood count</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 7.1 g/dL with breathlessness: one unit of irradiated, leukoreduced red cells today; platelets 22 without serious bleeding: no transfusion, as the biopsy is safe at this count</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Infection prophylaxis and the fever plan</td><td>From diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fever plan given in writing: 38.0 °C or more means the emergency department at once; thermometer at home</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV testing before therapy</td><td>Once, before therapy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hepatitis B surface antigen, core antibody and surface antibody, hepatitis C antibody and HIV test sent</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>HLA typing and allogeneic transplant evaluation</td><td>At diagnosis for any patient who may be a transplant candidate</td><td>Completed today</td><td>HLA typing sent</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Occupational exposure history and dose records</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>History documented; written request for records begun</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Diagnosis, classification and a treatment decision within two weeks of the first visit. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item><item>Complete cycle 1 without tumor lysis, invasive fungal infection, or bleeding. In progress; target 2026-10-30 [S]</item><item>Remission on the response assessment after cycle 1 (ELN 2022 complete remission, or complete remission with partial hematologic recovery). In progress; target 2026-10-30 [S]</item><item>Her own goals and values recorded and her symptoms controlled with the palliative care team from the first cycle. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item><item>Her occupational dose records obtained and the exposure history documented. In progress; target 2026-10-30 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Acute Myeloid Leukemia, Version 5.2026 (2026); Döhner H, et al. Diagnosis and management of AML in adults: 2022 recommendations from an international expert panel on behalf of the ELN. Blood 2022 (PMID 35797463) (2022); Khoury JD, et al. The 5th edition of the World Health Organization Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours: Myeloid and Histiocytic/Dendritic Neoplasms. Leukemia 2022 (PMID 35732831) (2022); Arber DA, et al. International Consensus Classification of Myeloid Neoplasms and Acute Leukemias: integrating morphologic, clinical, and genomic data. Blood 2022 (PMID 35767897) (2022); Carson JL, et al. Red Blood Cell Transfusion: 2023 AABB International Guidelines. JAMA 2023 (PMID 37824153) (2023); Kaufman RM, et al. Platelet transfusion: a clinical practice guideline from the AABB. Ann Intern Med 2015 (PMID 25383671) (2015); Hwang JP, et al. Hepatitis B Virus Screening and Management for Patients With Cancer Prior to Therapy: ASCO Provisional Clinical Opinion Update (PMID 32716741) (2020); 10 CFR 19.13, Notifications and reports to individuals (radiation exposure reports on request) (2026); 29 CFR 1910.1096, Ionizing radiation (records of exposure and disclosure to employees) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review when the cytogenetics and molecular results are back, in about ten days, with the echocardiogram and lung function tests. Blood count twice weekly with transfusion as needed. Emergency department at once for a temperature of 38.0 °C or more or bleeding that does not stop. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-28: genetic results, classification and risk, fitness for treatment, early palliative care, and day 1 of azacitidine with venetoclax. [S]</paragraph></text>

